  "findings": [
    "Round 1 passed 21/24 checks but failed the three batch cases because keyless inner commands were sent through validation that expected a top-level idempotency key.",
    "Round 2 introduced a broad read-path regression: validate() required key before recognizing snapshot/audit, which legitimately have no key. All 24 cumulative scenarios consequently failed; many successful mutation prefixes ended with an invalid read response. This count does not mean 24 independent business-logic defects.",
    "Rounds 3\u20135 retained that misplaced key check. Round 3 also omitted batch dispatch during refactoring; round 4 restored it. Round 5 changed no source code despite claiming completion.",
    "Round 6 moved snapshot/audit handling before the key requirement and separated keyless inner mutation validation from keyed top-level requests. All 24 visible checks then passed.",
    "Round 6 omitted control.json, so the harness did not finish yet. Round 7 preserved identical source and supplied a valid complete declaration with no remaining requirements.",
    "The harness rejected false completion in rounds 1, 4 and 5 and persisted through the regression. Final cumulative private evaluation passed all 30 cases after the entire project trajectory was frozen.",
    "This is concrete bounded-loop recovery evidence, including real source defects and a separate final control-only call. It does not establish that cheap sequential repair outperforms other policies or that recovery would occur without a sufficiently informative regression suite."
